    <Placemark>
      <name>People First Mid-South and Christchurch Groups</name>
      <description><![CDATA[Description: People First self-advocacy organisation for people with a learning disability (intellectual disability). Nothing about us, without us. The groups are run by and for people with a learning disability. At People First meetings people learn to speak up for themselves and to speak up for those people who cannot. Sometimes we invite people to come to speak to us about things we want to learn more about - like, the changes the Government might make to benefits in New Zealand. Sometimes the national and local government asks People First to help them make sure the best services are provided for people with learning disability - like how to make bus and train services easier and safer for us to use. In our groups we also meet new friends and have social times. We learn about things that are important to know about living with learning disability, that making mistakes is ok, our rights and responsibilities, our service providers, responsibilities and duty of care, making choices and gaining more confidence, making decisions and putting them into action. We also learn about the law and what it means to us and how to have our ideas heard by the Government, local council/community<br>Contact person: Margaret Matheson<br>phone number: 366-4210<br>email: marg@peoplefirst.org.nz<br>website (or Fb): http://www.facebook.com/peoplefirstnz  http://www.peoplefirst.org.nz/<br>Address: 301 Tuam Street, Christchurch Central, Christchurch 8011<br>Subject: Citizen and consumer, Community groups, Health and welfare, People with disabilities, People with mental disabilities, Personal development<br>Last update: 12/19/2018<br>X latitude: -43.5349752<br>Y longitude: 172.6475566]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#icon-1899-0288D1</styleUrl>
      <ExtendedData>
        <Data name="Description">
          <value>People First self-advocacy organisation for people with a learning disability (intellectual disability). Nothing about us, without us. The groups are run by and for people with a learning disability. At People First meetings people learn to speak up for themselves and to speak up for those people who cannot. Sometimes we invite people to come to speak to us about things we want to learn more about - like, the changes the Government might make to benefits in New Zealand. Sometimes the national and local government asks People First to help them make sure the best services are provided for people with learning disability - like how to make bus and train services easier and safer for us to use. In our groups we also meet new friends and have social times. We learn about things that are important to know about living with learning disability, that making mistakes is ok, our rights and responsibilities, our service providers, responsibilities and duty of care, making choices and gaining more confidence, making decisions and putting them into action. We also learn about the law and what it means to us and how to have our ideas heard by the Government, local council/community</value>
        </Data>
