    <Placemark>
      <name>Okains Bay Maori and Colonial Museum</name>
      <description><![CDATA[Description: Maori and colonial history come together in this extensive museum on Banks Peninsula. The Maori artefact building houses some of the rarer objects in the collection. These include a sacred god stick dating back to the year 1400, a war canoe dating back to 1867 and a valuable Akaroa hei tiki recovered in England and brought back to Okains Bay  by the museum's founder. Most of the carving on the magnificent meeting house was done by John Rua, a well-known New Zealand master carver. All traditions and tapu were observed. Colonial pioneering life is shown in displays in the Colonial Hall. There are also cottages, stables and working smithy, a print shop, many old horse-drawn gigs, carts and a saddlery. Traditional crafts and arts of both Maori and pakeha are demonstrated at Waitangi commemorations which are held annually on 6 February commemorating the birth of New Zealand as a nation<br>Contact person: Ian Day<br>phone number: 03 304-8611<br>email: info@okainsbaymuseum.co.nz<br>website (or Fb): http://www.okainsbaymuseum.co.nz/<br>Address: 1147 Okains Bay Road, Okains Bay 7583<br>Subject: Arts, crafts and hobbies, Historical museums, Maori -- Antiquities, Museums, Community groups, Pioneers -- Canterbury, Educational groups<br>Last update: 12/18/2018<br>X latitude: -43.7097143<br>Y longitude: 173.0415353]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#icon-1899-0288D1</styleUrl>
      <ExtendedData>
        <Data name="Description">
          <value><![CDATA[Maori and colonial history come together in this extensive museum on Banks Peninsula. The Maori artefact building houses some of the rarer objects in the collection. These include a sacred god stick dating back to the year 1400, a war canoe dating back to 1867 and a valuable Akaroa hei tiki recovered in England and brought back to Okains Bay  by the museum's founder. Most of the carving on the magnificent meeting house was done by John Rua, a well-known New Zealand master carver. All traditions and tapu were observed. Colonial pioneering life is shown in displays in the Colonial Hall. There are also cottages, stables and working smithy, a print shop, many old horse-drawn gigs, carts and a saddlery. Traditional crafts and arts of both Maori and pakeha are demonstrated at Waitangi commemorations which are held annually on 6 February commemorating the birth of New Zealand as a nation]]></value>
        </Data>
        <Data name="Contact person">
          <value>Ian Day</value>
        </Data>
        <Data name="phone number">
          <value>03 304-8611</value>
        </Data>
        <Data name="email">
          <value>info@okainsbaymuseum.co.nz</value>
        </Data>
